      section: "CS",
      q: "The chromatic number of a graph is the minimum number of colours used in a proper colouring of the graph. Let G be any graph with n vertices and chromatic number k. Which of the following statements is/are always TRUE? (A) G contains a complete subgraph with k vertices (B) G contains an independent set of size at least n/k (C) G contains at least k(k-1)/2 edges (D) G contains a vertex of degree at least k",
      options: ["G contains a complete subgraph with k vertices","G contains an independent set of size at least n/k","G contains at least k(k-1)/2 edges","G contains a vertex of degree at least k"],
      answers: [1,2],
      kind: "msq",
      marks: 2,
      explanation: "Partitioning the n vertices into k colour classes, by pigeonhole some class has at least n/k vertices, and being monochromatic it is an independent set (B). A classical result shows every k-chromatic graph has at least k(k-1)/2 edges, the minimum achieved by K_k (C). A graph can need k colours without containing a k-clique (A is false, e.g. odd cycles need 3 colours with no triangle), and needing k colours only forces some vertex of degree at least k-1, not k, so (D) is false (e.g. an odd cycle has max degree 2 but needs 3 colours)."
